<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n12351" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">dător</span></b> , ōris,
			m.
		id., <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b><i>a
				giver</i> (except in Plant. rare). </div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b> In gen., <i><span class="en">Plaut. Truc. 2, 1, 33</span></i>; <i><span class="en">2, 7,
				18</span></i>; fragm. ap. <i><span class="en">Prisc. p.
			616</span></i> P.: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">assit laetitiae Bacchus dator,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Verg.
					A. 1, 734</span></i>
			: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">hilarem enim datorem diligit Deus,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Vulg. 2
					Cor. 9, 7</span></i>
			.—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b>
			Esp. in playing ball, <i>the slave who hands the ball
				to the player</i>, opp. factor, the player himself, <i><span class="en">Plaut.
				Curc. 2, 3, 18</span></i>.</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
    <div class="alpheios-lex-alph-source">From <u>
            <u>A Latin Dictionary</u>
        </u> (Charlton T. Lewis, Charles Short, Oxford, Clarendon Press, 1879) </div>
